  THE QUESTION THE CAVEAT ANSWERS BADLY. 'At matched total shots the
  advantage is circuits, not shots' is a statement about ONE budget. Read
  the column instead: find the smallest budget at which V6 reaches the cos
  that parameter-shift needs its full budget to reach. The ratio of those
  two budgets is the shot-side advantage, and it is either there or it is
  not - one matched point cannot tell you.

  V6 is bias-limited well before it is shot-limited (v108: bias^2 3x
  variance at 43690 shots/circuit), so its cos should FLATTEN with budget
  while parameter-shift's keeps climbing. Where it flattens is the shot
  count V6 actually needs; everything above that is spent for nothing.

  Scope: one problem, one theta, one N, %d repeats, R swept over %s and the
  best reported - so V6 is given its best radius at every budget while
  parameter-shift has no equivalent knob. That favours V6 and is stated.
  R swept over: [0.3, 0.45, 0.6]
